    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration, which is done using the `delete` command. The Juniper command structure would be context-dependent based on where you\\'re configuring it (e.g., in edit interfaces ge-1/0/0 hierarchy), but the direct translation of the functionality is to remove the disable flag. \\n\\nIf you need the full Juniper-style configuration format (in set commands), it would typically look like:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 172.21.1.2/24\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 description \"MPLS interface\"\\n##\\n\\nBut the direct equivalent of \"no shutdown\" is the first answer above.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 172.21.1.2/24', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration, which is done using the `delete` command. The Juniper command structure would be context-dependent based on where you\\'re configuring it (e.g., in edit interfaces ge-1/0/0 hierarchy), but the direct translation of the functionality is to remove the disable flag. \\n\\nIf you need the full Juniper-style configuration format (in set commands), it would typically look like:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 172.21.1.2/24\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 description \"MPLS interface\"\\n##\\n\\nBut the direct equivalent of \"no shutdown\" is the first answer above.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 172.21.1.2/24'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 description \"MPLS interface\"', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration, which is done using the `delete` command. The Juniper command structure would be context-dependent based on where you\\'re configuring it (e.g., in edit interfaces ge-1/0/0 hierarchy), but the direct translation of the functionality is to remove the disable flag. \\n\\nIf you need the full Juniper-style configuration format (in set commands), it would typically look like:\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 172.21.1.2/24\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 description \"MPLS interface\"\\n##\\n\\nBut the direct equivalent of \"no shutdown\" is the first answer above.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 description \"MPLS interface\"'}]]": 1,
