    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Cisco, `no shutdown` enables an interface, while in Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default. To explicitly enable an interface (or ensure it's not disabled), you would remove any `disable` statement under the interface configuration using the `delete` command. The equivalent is to remove the `disable` flag from both interfaces.\\n\\nNote: Juniper interface names would typically use a different format (like ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0), but I preserved the original interface names as they appeared in the Cisco configuration for this translation.\", 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Cisco, `no shutdown` enables an interface, while in Juniper, interfaces are enabled by default. To explicitly enable an interface (or ensure it's not disabled), you would remove any `disable` statement under the interface configuration using the `delete` command. The equivalent is to remove the `disable` flag from both interfaces.\\n\\nNote: Juniper interface names would typically use a different format (like ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0), but I preserved the original interface names as they appeared in the Cisco configuration for this translation.\", 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet2/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
